NNAAMMEE
     wwhhiicchh — locate a program file (or files) in the path

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     wwhhiicchh [--aa] _n_a_m_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     wwhhiicchh takes a list of names and looks for the files which would be exe‐
     cuted had these names been given as commands.  Each argument is searched
     for along the user's path.

     If the --aa flag is given, wwhhiicchh will return a list of all matches instead
     of just the first match.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The following environment variables affect the execution of wwhhiicchh:

     PATH  A colon-separated list of directories in which to find executables.
           If PATH is not set or is empty, wwhhiicchh will use the system's default
           search path.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The wwhhiicchh utility exits with one of the following values:

           0       All names were successfully resolved.
           1       Some names were resolved but not all.
           2       No names were resolved.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     A diagnostic is given if an executable file with the argument name was
     not found in the path.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     csh(1), find(1), locate(1), whereis(1), environ(7)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     wwhhiicchh also exists as a built-in to csh(1), though with a different syn‐
     tax.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A wwhhiicchh command first appeared in 3.0BSD.

     The --aa option first appeared in OpenBSD 2.3.

